from (values
('127.110.d.5.L',3,'Box supports include bar hangers, brackets, and:','["Tape", "String", "Threaded rod supports", "Glue sticks"]'::jsonb,2,'Hangers hold boxes.','original'),
('127.110.d.5.L',4,'A ceiling box between joists is often supported by a:','["Rope", "Paper clip", "Drywall", "Adjustable bar hanger"]'::jsonb,3,'Bar hangers span joists.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.A',3,'Conductors can be installed in conduit by pushing them or by:','["Pulling them with a pull line", "Throwing them", "Taping to a vehicle", "Heating them"]'::jsonb,0,'Pull lines are standard.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.A',4,'For long runs with large conductors, pulling is usually done with a:','["Bare hand", "Mechanical puller", "Paper rope", "Kite string"]'::jsonb,1,'Machines reduce effort.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.B',3,'Before a cable pull, the crew should:','["Block the exits", "Start without a plan", "Plan the route, equipment, and communication", "Skip safety"]'::jsonb,2,'Planning prevents damage.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.B',4,'A pulling plan should identify the maximum allowed:','["Paint finish", "Wall color", "Truck brand", "Pulling tension"]'::jsonb,3,'Tension limits protect cable.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.C',3,'Heavy cable reels are moved safely using:','["Proper lifting equipment and reel trailers", "Rope only", "Bare hands", "Rolling down stairs"]'::jsonb,0,'Reels are heavy.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.C',4,'When rolling a reel, the correct direction is the one shown by the:','["Driver''s choice", "Arrow on the reel flange", "Manufacturer logo", "Color of the cable"]'::jsonb,1,'Arrows prevent loosening.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.D',3,'Reel stands and spindles allow the reel to:','["Burn", "Stand still", "Turn freely as cable is pulled", "Lock"]'::jsonb,2,'Free turning prevents tangles.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.D',4,'The reel should be positioned so the cable pays off the:','["Side onto the floor", "Bottom in a heap", "Center out", "Top of the reel in line with the conduit"]'::jsonb,3,'Alignment prevents kinks.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.E',3,'A mandrel is pulled through a conduit to:','["Check for obstructions or damage", "Seal the end", "Color the conduit", "Add voltage"]'::jsonb,0,'A mandrel proves the path.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.E',4,'A swab or brush is used to:','["Bend the pipe", "Clean debris and moisture from conduit", "Cut wires", "Paint the inside"]'::jsonb,1,'Clean conduit protects insulation.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.F',3,'A pull line can be installed in conduit by blowing a lightweight line with:','["Electric shock", "Hammer blows", "Compressed air and a foam piston", "Water pumps"]'::jsonb,2,'A piston carries the line.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.F',4,'A pull line for a heavy cable should be strong enough for the expected:','["Weight of the box", "Age of the building", "Color", "Pulling tension"]'::jsonb,3,'Rope rating matters.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.G',3,'A power fish tape system helps to:','["Push or pull a line through long conduit runs", "Measure voltage", "Paint walls", "Cut wire"]'::jsonb,0,'Power fish tapes help routing.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.G',4,'Before using a power fish tape, check that the conduit is:','["Wet", "De-energized and free of live conductors", "Locked", "Full of wires"]'::jsonb,1,'Avoid contact with live parts.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.H',3,'The end of a conductor is prepared for pulling by attaching a:','["Plug", "Label", "Pulling eye or grip", "Lamp"]'::jsonb,2,'Grips connect to the line.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.H',4,'A basket grip on a cable end holds by:','["Magnets", "Melting", "Glue", "Tightening as tension increases"]'::jsonb,3,'Mesh squeezes the cable.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.I',3,'A cable puller can be powered by an electric motor or:','["Hydraulics", "A wind-up key", "A candle", "A flashlight"]'::jsonb,0,'Pullers vary.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.I',4,'A tugger is a small puller commonly used to:','["Weld pipe", "Pull cable through conduit", "Test voltage", "Cut conduit"]'::jsonb,1,'Tuggers pull.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.J',3,'High-force cable pulling is used for:','["Phone wires", "Doorbell wires", "Large, heavy cables in long runs", "Lamp cords"]'::jsonb,2,'Heavy loads need force.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.J',4,'During a high-force pull, a tension monitor helps to:','["Add speed", "Reduce voltage", "Change color", "Stay below the cable''s maximum pulling tension"]'::jsonb,3,'Monitors prevent overload.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.K',3,'In a vertical conduit run, conductors are supported to prevent:','["Their own weight from stressing the cable", "Cooling", "Rain", "Painting"]'::jsonb,0,'NEC requires support.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.K',4,'Cable supports in a vertical run are installed at intervals set by the:','["Truck brand", "NEC based on conductor size", "Color", "Installer''s mood"]'::jsonb,1,'Spacing is code-based.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.L',3,'In a cable tray, power conductors are often laid in rather than:','["Placed loose", "Tied with tape", "Pulled through a closed pipe", "Laid down flat"]'::jsonb,2,'Tray cable is laid in.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.L',4,'When pulling cable in a tray, rollers are used to:','["Cut cable", "Add friction", "Melt cable", "Reduce friction and protect the jacket"]'::jsonb,3,'Rollers ease pulls.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.M',3,'Good communication during a pull helps the crew to:','["Coordinate speed and stop if there is a problem", "Work in silence", "Skip checking", "Ignore each other"]'::jsonb,0,'Radios help.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.M',4,'If someone at the feed end sees a jam, they should:','["Leave the area", "Call out to stop the pull immediately", "Pull harder", "Wait silently"]'::jsonb,1,'Stop before damage.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.N',3,'Pulling tension is calculated using the cable weight, the coefficient of friction, and the:','["Noise", "Brand", "Length and bends of the run", "Color"]'::jsonb,2,'Geometry affects tension.','original'),
('127.110.d.6.N',4,'Adding more bends to a conduit run makes the pulling tension generally:','["Stay unchanged", "Fall to zero", "Become negative", "Climb"]'::jsonb,3,'Bends multiply tension.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.A',3,'A cable tray assembly is made of straight sections, fittings, and:','["Supports", "Drain pipes", "Paint cans", "Gutters"]'::jsonb,0,'Tray needs supports.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.A',4,'Cable tray rungs or solid bottoms support the:','["Ceiling tiles", "Cables laid in the tray", "Light fixtures only", "Water pipes"]'::jsonb,1,'Trays carry cable.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.B',3,'Cable trays are hung from the ceiling using:','["String", "Tape", "Threaded rod hangers", "Glue"]'::jsonb,2,'Rods suspend trays.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.B',4,'Hanger spacing for a cable tray depends on the tray''s:','["Color", "Brand of paint", "Owner''s name", "Load rating and span"]'::jsonb,3,'Load limits spacing.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.C',3,'Cable enters and leaves a tray through a:','["Conduit or cable fitting at the side or bottom", "Wall mirror", "Window", "Door hinge"]'::jsonb,0,'Entry fittings protect cable.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.C',4,'A bushing or fitting at a tray entrance protects the cable from:','["Cool air", "Sharp edges", "Sunlight", "Rain only"]'::jsonb,1,'Edges can cut jackets.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.D',3,'A horizontal elbow in a tray changes the run:','["Color", "Voltage", "Direction in the same plane", "Weight"]'::jsonb,2,'Elbows turn.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.D',4,'A vertical tray fitting is used to move the tray:','["Into the wall", "Sideways only", "Underground", "Up or down between levels"]'::jsonb,3,'Vertical elbows change elevation.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.E',3,'NEMA standards for cable trays cover tray:','["Load classes and dimensions", "Song lists", "Shoe sizes", "Paint brands"]'::jsonb,0,'NEMA VE1 sets classes.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.E',4,'The NEMA load rating of a tray tells the:','["Color of the tray", "Weight per foot it can support on a given span", "Age", "Cost per mile"]'::jsonb,1,'Ratings guide selection.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.F',3,'NEC rules for cable tray require that cables be:','["Bare wire only", "Painted", "Of types permitted for tray use", "Any wire"]'::jsonb,2,'Use tray-rated cable.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.F',4,'Where cable tray passes through a wall, the opening must be:','["Covered with paper", "Left open", "Sealed with tape", "Firestopped as required"]'::jsonb,3,'Firestopping maintains rating.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.G',3,'A metal cable tray can serve as an equipment grounding conductor if it is:','["Listed and properly bonded across joints", "Loose", "Unlisted", "Painted"]'::jsonb,0,'Continuity matters.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.G',4,'Bonding jumpers are installed across tray expansion joints to:','["Add noise", "Keep electrical continuity", "Cool the cable", "Allow breaks"]'::jsonb,1,'Jumpers bridge gaps.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.H',3,'Working drawings show cable tray fittings with:','["Recipes", "Songs", "Symbols and dimensions", "Photographs of lunch"]'::jsonb,2,'Drawings use symbols.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.H',4,'A drawing note for tray elevation helps installers know the:','["Color", "Cost of lunch", "Brand", "Height above finished floor"]'::jsonb,3,'Elevation is shown on plan.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.I',3,'To size a tray, the installer adds up the cross-sectional area of the cables and compares it with the tray''s:','["Allowable fill area", "Color", "Weight in grams", "Age"]'::jsonb,0,'NEC limits fill.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.I',4,'If cables fill more than the allowable area in a tray, the installer should:','["Squash the cables", "Choose a wider or additional tray", "Remove the cover", "Ignore the limit"]'::jsonb,1,'Fill rules protect cable.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.J',3,'Rollers and sheaves in a tray reduce friction and:','["Add tension", "Slow the job", "Protect cable from damage during a pull", "Cause scraping"]'::jsonb,2,'They guide cable.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.J',4,'The size of a sheave should match the cable''s:','["Brand", "Price", "Color", "Minimum bending radius"]'::jsonb,3,'Sheaves avoid sharp bends.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.K',3,'Rollers are placed in a tray at changes of direction and:','["At regular intervals along straight runs", "Nowhere", "Only at the ends", "In the roof"]'::jsonb,0,'Intervals keep cable off tray.','original'),
('127.110.d.7.K',4,'A roller should be placed at the outside of a horizontal turn to:','["Hold the lights", "Guide the cable around the curve", "Stop the pull", "Cut cable"]'::jsonb,1,'Rollers guide turns.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.A',3,'A good conductor termination is one that is:','["Taped over", "Loose", "Clean, tight, and made with proper tools", "Dirty"]'::jsonb,2,'Quality connections prevent heat.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.A',4,'A poor termination typically causes:','["Extra voltage", "Cool wire", "Lower resistance", "Heat at the connection"]'::jsonb,3,'Resistance makes heat.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.B',3,'Before a termination, the cable end is prepared by:','["Removing insulation to the proper length", "Soaking it", "Cutting the conductor in half", "Painting it"]'::jsonb,0,'Strip to length.','original')
