from (values
('127.110.d.8.B',4,'When stripping insulation, the installer must avoid:','["Wearing gloves", "Nicking the conductor", "Measuring", "Using strippers"]'::jsonb,1,'Nicks weaken wire.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.C',3,'A lug is installed on a conductor by:','["Taping", "Knotting", "Inserting the stripped end and securing it", "Gluing"]'::jsonb,2,'Lugs connect wire to equipment.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.C',4,'Large lugs are commonly attached to conductors using a:','["Hammer", "Saw", "Pencil", "Compression crimp tool"]'::jsonb,3,'Crimping makes a solid joint.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.D',3,'Training cable at a termination point means:','["Shaping it neatly to reach its terminal without strain", "Cutting it short", "Burying it", "Painting it"]'::jsonb,0,'Neat routing reduces stress.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.D',4,'When training cable, the installer should maintain the correct:','["Voltage of lamps", "Bending radius", "Brand", "Wall color"]'::jsonb,1,'Avoid sharp bends.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.E',3,'The NEC requires terminations to be rated for the conductor:','["Brand only", "Smell", "Temperature and material", "Color only"]'::jsonb,2,'Terminals have ratings.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.E',4,'If a terminal is rated for copper only, the installer must not use:','["Any wire", "Copper conductors", "Insulated wire", "Aluminum conductors"]'::jsonb,3,'Material compatibility.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.F',3,'Mechanical stress on a termination can:','["Loosen the connection over time", "Improve it", "Seal it", "Cool it"]'::jsonb,0,'Stress damages joints.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.F',4,'Pulling the cable tight against a lug may:','["Reduce current", "Strain the termination and cause failure", "Remove heat", "Strengthen it"]'::jsonb,1,'Slack prevents strain.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.G',3,'Bolting a lug requires torque that is:','["Zero", "Maximum possible", "Specified by the manufacturer", "Random"]'::jsonb,2,'Use the specified torque.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.G',4,'Using a torque wrench on lug bolts prevents:','["Cooling", "Correct torque", "Paint damage", "Loose or overtightened connections"]'::jsonb,3,'Both extremes cause failure.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.H',3,'Crimping squeezes a connector onto a conductor to form a:','["Solid mechanical and electrical connection", "Spring", "Hole", "Weak joint"]'::jsonb,0,'Crimps hold tightly.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.H',4,'The crimp die must match the connector and the:','["Paint", "Conductor size", "Voltage of the sun", "Truck"]'::jsonb,1,'Dies are sized.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.I',3,'Choosing a lug requires knowing the conductor size and the:','["Date", "Color of the room", "Conductor material", "Lunch menu"]'::jsonb,2,'Material matters.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.I',4,'A lug marked AL9CU is suitable for:','["Copper only", "Aluminum only", "Neither", "Both aluminum and copper conductors"]'::jsonb,3,'AL9CU is dual-rated.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.J',3,'A splice joins two conductors to make:','["One continuous conductor", "A switch", "A motor", "A fuse"]'::jsonb,0,'Splices join.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.J',4,'Splices must be made in an approved:','["Soil", "Box or enclosure that stays accessible", "Buried vault with no access", "Wall cavity"]'::jsonb,1,'Splices need access.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.K',3,'A hydraulic crimper is typically used for:','["Small lamp wire", "Doorbell wire", "Large lugs and connectors", "Phone wire"]'::jsonb,2,'Hydraulics give force.','original'),
('127.110.d.8.K',4,'Before crimping, the tool should be checked for the correct:','["Brand sticker", "Paint color", "Handle color", "Die and pressure setting"]'::jsonb,3,'Proper setup is essential.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.A',3,'A commercial building with high electrical demand is typically served by a:','["Three-phase service", "Single outlet", "Solar toy", "Battery pack"]'::jsonb,0,'Three-phase suits large loads.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.A',4,'Industrial plants often have services at a higher voltage such as:','["12 volts", "480 volts", "5 volts", "1 volt"]'::jsonb,1,'Larger systems use 480 V.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.B',3,'A one-line diagram shows the service as:','["A photo", "A recipe", "A single line representing the entire system", "Song lyrics"]'::jsonb,2,'One-lines simplify.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.B',4,'On a one-line diagram, the main disconnect appears:','["After the lights", "Underground only", "On the roof", "Between the utility source and the distribution equipment"]'::jsonb,3,'It is at the service entrance.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.C',3,'Service-entrance equipment is selected based on calculated:','["Load", "Height", "Brand of paint", "Color"]'::jsonb,0,'Load determines size.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.C',4,'A 400-ampere, 480-volt, three-phase service can supply about how many kVA?','["900 kVA", "332 kVA", "192 kVA", "48 kVA"]'::jsonb,1,'1.732 x 480 x 400 = 332,000 VA.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.D',3,'The NEC article that covers services is:','["Article 90 only", "Article 1", "Article 230", "Article 700"]'::jsonb,2,'Services are Article 230.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.D',4,'The NEC requires each service to have a:','["Doorbell", "Fountain", "Mailbox", "Disconnecting means"]'::jsonb,3,'A disconnect is required.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.E',3,'A main disconnect switch is used to:','["Shut off all power to the building", "Cool a motor", "Light a room", "Pump water"]'::jsonb,0,'Main switch removes service.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.E',4,'Panelboards contain overcurrent devices for the:','["Roof", "Branch circuits and feeders", "Pipes", "Windows"]'::jsonb,1,'Panels distribute power.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.F',3,'To plan a panel, the electrician lists the connected loads and the:','["Wall colors", "Paint brands", "Number of circuits needed", "Window sizes"]'::jsonb,2,'Circuits follow loads.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.F',4,'A continuous load is one expected to run for:','["One minute", "Two minutes", "Ten seconds", "Three hours or more"]'::jsonb,3,'NEC defines it as 3 hours.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.G',3,'Service grounding connects the system to the earth through:','["A grounding electrode system", "A mailbox", "A light bulb", "A shingle"]'::jsonb,0,'Electrodes tie to earth.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.G',4,'At the service, the neutral and the equipment grounding conductors are:','["Kept separate forever", "Bonded together", "Removed", "Cut"]'::jsonb,1,'Bonded at the main.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.H',3,'The NEC requires GFCI protection in locations such as:','["Bedroom closets", "Attic floors", "Bathrooms and rooftops", "Hallway ceilings"]'::jsonb,2,'Wet or hazardous areas.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.H',4,'A GFCI trips when it senses a difference of about how many milliamperes between hot and neutral?','["50 amperes", "500 milliamperes", "5 amperes", "5 milliamperes"]'::jsonb,3,'Class A trips near 5 mA.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.I',3,'A single-phase three-wire service supplies 120 volts and:','["240 volts", "1000 volts", "480 volts", "12 volts"]'::jsonb,0,'Residential split-phase.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.I',4,'The neutral in a single-phase three-wire system carries:','["Double current", "Only the unbalanced current between legs", "No current ever", "All current"]'::jsonb,1,'Balanced legs cancel.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.J',3,'A wye three-phase service has a:','["Single winding", "Battery", "Neutral point where the three windings join", "Triangle shape with no neutral"]'::jsonb,2,'Wye has a center point.','original'),
('127.110.d.9.J',4,'A 208Y/120-volt wye system provides 120 volts line-to-neutral and 208 volts:','["Ground-to-pipe", "Neutral-to-ground", "Motor-to-wall", "Line-to-line"]'::jsonb,3,'208 = 120 x 1.732.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.A',3,'Overcurrent protection is needed to prevent:','["Damage from excessive current", "Better sound", "Nice lighting", "Lower bills"]'::jsonb,0,'Protects wires and equipment.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.A',4,'Without overcurrent protection, a short circuit could cause:','["Longer life", "Fire or equipment destruction", "Better efficiency", "Cooler wires"]'::jsonb,1,'Large currents heat wires.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.B',3,'The interrupting rating of a breaker is the maximum:','["Weight", "Voltage of the sun", "Fault current it can safely interrupt", "Color"]'::jsonb,2,'Rating must exceed available fault current.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.B',4,'A fuse''s ampere rating is the current it can carry:','["With no load", "For one second only", "Only on Sundays", "Continuously without opening"]'::jsonb,3,'Ratings are continuous.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.C',3,'A thermal-magnetic circuit breaker trips on overloads by means of a:','["Bimetal strip that heats and bends", "Magnet only", "Fuse link", "Spring only"]'::jsonb,0,'Thermal element handles overloads.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.C',4,'The magnetic part of a breaker responds to:','["Slow overloads only", "Large short-circuit currents instantly", "Voltage dips", "Cold weather"]'::jsonb,1,'Magnetic trip is fast.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.D',3,'Selecting an overcurrent device requires considering the conductor ampacity and the:','["Time of day", "Owner''s mood", "Load type", "Paint"]'::jsonb,2,'Match device to load.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.D',4,'A breaker protecting a 12 AWG copper branch circuit is limited by the NEC to:','["100 amperes", "60 amperes", "200 amperes", "20 amperes"]'::jsonb,3,'12 AWG small-conductor rule.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.E',3,'In a motor circuit, a dual-element time-delay fuse lets the circuit tolerate:','["Brief motor-starting surges without opening", "Instant opening always", "No overload", "Reverse flow"]'::jsonb,0,'Dual-element handles motors.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.E',4,'A single-element fuse differs from a time-delay fuse in that it:','["Resists surges", "Opens quickly on overloads", "Never opens", "Is rechargeable"]'::jsonb,1,'No delay.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.F',3,'GFCIs can save lives by detecting:','["Sound", "Temperature of wire", "Small leakage currents to ground", "Light levels"]'::jsonb,2,'They detect ground faults.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.F',4,'A GFCI protects people but does not protect against:','["Leakage to ground", "Ground faults", "Wet-area shock", "Shock from touching both hot and neutral"]'::jsonb,3,'Hot-to-neutral contact is not detected.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.G',3,'Available short-circuit current at a point depends mainly on the source and the circuit:','["Impedance", "Color", "Length of tape", "Weight"]'::jsonb,0,'Lower impedance, higher current.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.G',4,'If a transformer secondary is 480 V with an impedance of 0.1 ohm per phase, the approximate fault current is:','["48 amperes", "2,771 amperes", "4,800 amperes", "27 amperes"]'::jsonb,1,'(480/1.732)/0.1 is about 2,771.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.H',3,'A breaker that trips repeatedly should be:','["Ignored", "Replaced with a larger one", "Investigated for an overload or fault", "Taped closed"]'::jsonb,2,'Find the cause.','original'),
('127.110.d.10.H',4,'Routine maintenance on breakers includes:','["Soaking them", "Hammering them", "Painting them", "Exercising them and checking connections"]'::jsonb,3,'Maintenance prevents failure.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
