('127.110.d.11.A',3,'A contactor is used to:','["Switch a power circuit using a control signal", "Cut conductors", "Cool wires", "Measure voltage"]'::jsonb,0,'Contactors switch loads.','original'),
('127.110.d.11.A',4,'A relay differs from a contactor mainly because a relay is generally used for:','["Power plants", "Lower-current control switching", "Heavy motor loads", "Welding"]'::jsonb,1,'Relays are for light loads.','original'),
('127.110.d.11.B',3,'To select a contactor, you must know the load voltage, current, and the:','["Wall color", "Truck", "Coil voltage", "Height"]'::jsonb,2,'Coil must match control.','original'),
('127.110.d.11.B',4,'A contactor for a motor must be rated for the motor''s:','["Color", "Brand", "Paint", "Horsepower or full-load current"]'::jsonb,3,'Ratings match the load.','original'),
('127.110.d.11.C',3,'A mechanical contactor closes its contacts when its coil is:','["Energized", "Open", "Cold", "Painted"]'::jsonb,0,'Coil pulls contacts closed.','original'),
('127.110.d.11.C',4,'When a contactor coil is de-energized, a spring:','["Closes the contacts", "Opens the contacts", "Burns the coil", "Reverses flow"]'::jsonb,1,'Springs return contacts.','original'),
('127.110.d.11.D',3,'A solid-state contactor switches with:','["Moving blades", "Springs", "Semiconductor devices instead of moving contacts", "Water"]'::jsonb,2,'No moving parts.','original'),
('127.110.d.11.D',4,'One advantage of a solid-state contactor is:','["Visible arcing", "Contact bounce", "Loud clicking", "Silent, fast switching"]'::jsonb,3,'No arcing.','original'),
('127.110.d.11.E',3,'The NEC requires contactors and relays to be installed:','["In suitable enclosures and as listed", "Anywhere", "Outdoors unprotected", "Unlabeled"]'::jsonb,0,'Follow listing.','original'),
('127.110.d.11.E',4,'Contactors installed outdoors need an enclosure rated for:','["Shade", "Weather exposure", "Paint", "Silence"]'::jsonb,1,'Outdoor ratings.','original'),
('127.110.d.11.F',3,'A lighting contactor is often used to control:','["Phones", "One doorbell", "Large groups of lights at once", "Radios"]'::jsonb,2,'Lighting contactors switch banks.','original'),
('127.110.d.11.F',4,'Lighting contactors can be operated by a time clock or a:','["Pen", "Hammer", "Radio brand", "Photocell"]'::jsonb,3,'Automated controls.','original'),
('127.110.d.11.G',3,'An overload relay protects a motor from:','["Prolonged overcurrent", "Paint", "Sound", "Light"]'::jsonb,0,'Heater elements sense current.','original'),
('127.110.d.11.G',4,'When an overload relay trips, it opens the:','["Lights", "Control circuit to drop out the contactor", "Water line", "Phone line"]'::jsonb,1,'It breaks the coil circuit.','original'),
('127.110.d.11.H',3,'A simple start-stop control circuit uses a normally closed stop button and a normally open:','["Lamp", "Ground", "Start button", "Fuse"]'::jsonb,2,'Start and stop pair.','original'),
